.TH SYS_CHILD_PORT 2
.SH NAME
sys_child_port \- learn of ended children in a message loop (SYS_CHILD_PORT)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_child_port(long port);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_child_port
names one of the caller's ports where the kernel tells it that a child
has ended: a "token 0" message (as for interrupts) whose
.I w[1]
has
.B NOTE_CHILD
set. A server so learns of it in its own
.BR ipc_recv (2)
loop, then collects the children with
.BR sys_wait (2)
and
.BR WAIT_NOHANG .
Several ends may arrive as one message. 0 stops the messages.
.PP
The supervisor,
.BR init (8),
uses it for the servers it restarts, for the processes it adopts, and for
.B NOTE_WANT
(an on-demand server is wanted,
.BR sys_want (2)).
.SH RETURN VALUE
0, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I port
is not one of the caller's.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_wait (2),
.BR ipc_recv (2),
.BR sys_want (2),
.BR init (8)
